<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  behavior_tree.xml (problem4)

  Deliberately the SIMPLEST possible reactive-redescend tree this
  theory can express: no obstacle geometry anywhere, exactly ONE
  reactive trigger (battery_below), and ONE fallback with two plain
  branches. Built for the performance investigation into why
  the theory's exact inference blows up once a fallback_node's
  branching gets chained across two or more hops: this problem
  isolates that mechanism from every other variable (obstacle count,
  polygon-restriction geometry, guard style) that earlier testing
  already ruled out one at a time.

  Fallback (plain, non-reactive - BatteryFallback):
    ReactiveSequence (branch 1, TryGoal - battery allows it):
      BatteryOver threshold=70 : only try the goal leg if battery is
        currently above 70%, checked FRESH each descent (a live state
        check, not history-based), so it correctly fails on its own
        once battery drops below 70% partway through branch 1's walk.
      PlanStraight goal=22.275;2.075
      MoveTo triggers=collision;battery;battery_below(70)
    Sequence (plain - branch 2: go home):
      PlanStraight goal=2.275;2.075
      MoveTo triggers=collision;battery

  Branch 1's own battery_below(70) trigger token carries the code of
  its own NEAREST enclosing reactive composite - here that's the
  ReactiveSequence "TryGoal" itself, one level up. Firing it makes
  ONLY that ReactiveSequence restart its own children (fresh, from
  BatteryOver) rather than redescending the whole tree: BatteryOver
  now reads the CURRENT (already-below-70%) battery level and fails
  immediately, so TryGoal (still non-reactive as far as its own
  parent is concerned) simply fails, and the outer plain
  BatteryFallback does what a plain fallback always does - falls
  through to branch 2 (GoHome). Net effect is identical to the old
  whole-tree redescend for this particular tree shape (unlike
  problem3's obstacle_on_path, battery level is a simple state fluent
  that's always meaningfully checkable, live, with no "was there ever
  a walk in progress" precondition to work around), which is why this
  problem is also used as the regression check that localized
  redescend reproduces the prior global-redescend results.

  Start position (2.275,2.075, this problem's own config.yaml) IS the
  "home" this tree returns to; the goal (22.275,2.075) is 20 metres
  east of it, tuned together with config.yaml's raised
  moving_drain_rate (5.0%/time-unit, vs. problem0-3's shared 0.5) so
  the walk crosses the 70% threshold well before reaching the goal.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Fallback name="BatteryFallback">
      <ReactiveSequence name="TryGoal">
        <BatteryOver threshold="70"/>
        <PlanStraight goal="22.275;2.075" control_points="{cp}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp}" triggers="collision;battery;battery_below(70)"/>
      </ReactiveSequence>
      <Sequence name="GoHome">
        <PlanStraight goal="2.275;2.075" control_points="{cp2}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp2}" triggers="collision;battery"/>
      </Sequence>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>

  <TreeNodesModel>
    <Action ID="MoveTo">
      <input_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <input_port name="triggers" type="std::vector&lt;std::string&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Action ID="PlanStraight">
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
      <output_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Condition ID="BatteryOver">
      <input_port name="threshold" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
  </TreeNodesModel>
</root>
